          "case_name": "Doe 30's Mother v. Bradley",
          "citation": "58 A.3d 429 (Del. Super. Ct. 2012)",
          "court": "Superior Court of Delaware",
          "year": 2012,
          "date_decided": "2012-03-29",
          "holding": "The Superior Court dismissed the common-law duty claims against physicians and the Medical Society, holding that Delaware's Medical Practice Act and Child Abuse Prevention Act reporting statutes do not create a new affirmative tort duty to report and cannot support negligence per se absent a pre-existing common-law duty.",
          "relevance": "Arises directly from physicians who suspected a colleague's abuse of child patients and did not report.",
          "quote": "the Court will not recognize a new affirmative common law tort duty stemming from the statutory language of the MPA or CAPA",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5346326/doe-30s-mother-v-bradley/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5346326
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Hunt v. Division of Family Services",
          "citation": "146 A.3d 1051 (Del. 2015)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Delaware",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-09-16",
          "holding": "On expedited interlocutory appeal the Court affirmed, holding that the Family Court's statutory authority to consent to medical care for a child in DFS custody carries with it the authority to de-escalate treatment and withdraw life support, and that both parents received procedural due process.",
          "relevance": "Arises from a hospital's identification of non-accidental trauma and defines the court's role once medical findings enter the dependency case.",
          "quote": "Accordingly, we hold that the Family Court's authority to de-escalate medical treatment and to withdraw life support is a logical corollary to its statutory authority to consent to medical care.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2959591/hunt-v-division-of-family-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2959591
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Division of Family Services v. A.B.",
          "citation": "980 A.2d 1045 (Del. Fam. Ct. 2009)",
          "court": "Delaware Family Court",
          "year": 2009,
          "date_decided": "2009-06-25",
          "holding": "The Family Court held a substance-abuse treatment provider in civil contempt and fined it for failing to produce a mother's records in response to the court's good-cause disclosure order and subpoena duces tecum, notwithstanding the federal confidentiality rules governing such records.",
          "relevance": "Governs how a treatment provider's records reach a dependency case and the releases and good-cause orders required.",
          "quote": "Furthermore, in a civil contempt proceeding, it is not a defense that the party in contempt did not have subjective intent to violate the Court's Order.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2306069/division-of-family-services-v-ab/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2306069
        }
